          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Humanitarian Work</strong><br>\nDiana was renowned for her humanitarian work, which began shortly after she became Princess of Wales. She was a patron of over 100 charities, focusing on issues ranging from leprosy to domestic violence. She famously walked through an active landmine field in Angola to raise awareness for the Halo Trust. Diana's ability to connect with the sick and suffering, often breaking royal protocol to hug or hold hands with them, earned her the affectionate nickname \"The People's Princess.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Motherhood</strong><br>\nDiana was a devoted mother to her two sons, Prince William and Prince Harry. She sought to give them as normal an upbringing as possible, taking them to amusement parks, fast-food restaurants, and on public transport. Her approach to motherhood was considered unconventional for royal standards, as she took her sons to shelters to show them life beyond the palace walls, instilling in them a sense of duty to the less fortunate.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Struggles</strong><br>\nThroughout her marriage, Diana faced personal struggles that became public knowledge. She dealt with bulimia and depression, which were largely attributed to the pressures of royal life and her unhappy marriage. Her difficulties were heightened by intense media scrutiny and her feeling that the royal family did not support her.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
