          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Separation and Divorce</strong><br>\nDiana and Charles's separation was announced in December 1992, and they divorced in 1996. Following the separation, Diana retained her title as \"Princess of Wales\" but was no longer referred to as \"Her Royal Highness.\" After the divorce, she continued her charity work and maintained her high public profile as a member of the British royal family.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Media and Public Attention</strong><br>\nThe media were intensely fascinated with Diana, often intruding into her private life. Despite the intrusion, Diana used her celebrity to benefit her charitable work but also struggled with the lack of privacy. The paparazzi followed her relentlessly, contributing to the public's interest in her life and ultimately leading to controversy over their role in her death.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nDiana died on August 31, 1997, in a car crash in Paris while the driver was fleeing from paparazzi. Her death prompted an unprecedented outpouring of grief worldwide. Diana left a lasting legacy through her charitable works and her approach to royal duty, which has influenced the modernization of the monarchy and the way royal family members interact with the public and press.</p>\n"
              },
